ROLE: novelty_killer  VOTE: yellow

CLOSEST_PRIOR_WORK:
- {cite: "Singhal, Buckley, Mitra (1996), Pivoted Document Length Normalization (SIGIR)", url: "https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/243199.243206", why_close: "The entire 'recovery arm' (long templated distractors get crushed) IS pivoted length normalization — the b>0 penalty on long docs. This is the load-bearing mechanism of the claimed inverted-U and is textbook."}
- {cite: "Robertson & Zaragoza (2009), The Probabilistic Relevance Framework: BM25 and Beyond", url: "https://www.nowpublishers.com/article/Details/INR-019", why_close: "TF saturation (k1) + length normalization (b) jointly imply that stuffing query terms into a doc helps until the length penalty overtakes the TF gain — the rising-then-falling distractor score is a direct consequence, not a new effect."}
- {cite: "Zhong et al. (2023), Poisoning Retrieval Corpora by Injecting Adversarial Passages (EMNLP)", url: "https://aclanthology.org/2023.emnlp-main.849/", why_close: "Adversarial query-term injection into corpus documents to manipulate ranking; same family as 'template injects query terms into distractors,' though it PROMOTES attackers rather than framing demotion of a clean doc. Not a non-monotone-length result."}
- {cite: "Gospodinov, MacAvaney, Macdonald (2023), Doc2Query--: When Less is More", url: "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-28238-6_31", why_close: "Over-expansion of a document hurts first-stage retrieval — the 'more added text eventually backfires' intuition, on the target doc and quality-driven rather than a length-axis inverted-U on a third party."}
- {cite: "Collapse of Dense Retrievers (2503.05037)", url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.05037", why_close: "Verified real and as the packet describes — STATIC length/literal biases, NOT a non-monotone preprocessing response. Correctly excluded as a collision."}

NAMING_VS_MECHANISM: Mostly a novel OBSERVATION of a non-novel mechanism, not a new mechanism. Both arms decompose into known BM25 properties: rising arm = trivial TF gain from query-term injection; recovery arm = pivoted length normalization (Singhal 1996). The researcher's own honest nuance is decisive here — qterm_idf is FLAT across L>0, so the claim's stated second half ("at large L … spreads the query-terms' df so their IDF collapses") does NOT operate on the L axis; the L-driven recovery is purely length-norm. So the headline "length-norm vs IDF-collapse crossover" is overstated: along L it is length-norm-vs-TF-injection, i.e. a re-derivation of BM25 length normalization defeating term stuffing. The genuine, unoccupied delta is narrow: the specific OBSERVABLE — a *clean third-party* doc's rank tracing an inverted-U as a *competitor's* boilerplate length grows ("corpus-hygiene danger zone on competitor docs"). I found no source reporting that specific observable.

FATAL_COLLISIONS: None verified. A KILL requires the same phenomenon in ≥2 independent sources; I could not find even one source reporting the clean-answer-rank inverted-U vs competitor-template-length. The mechanism is known but the specific framed observable appears unoccupied → no fatal collision, hence not RED.

MANDATORY_RELATED_WORK:
- Singhal et al. 1996 (pivoted length normalization) and Robertson & Zaragoza 2009 (BM25/k1/b) — MUST cite; the inverted-U must be positioned as a compositional consequence of these, not a new mechanism.
- Corpus-poisoning / adversarial-passage line: Zhong et al. 2023 (EMNLP 2023.emnlp-main.849), and at minimum one recent multi-attacker work (PoisonArena, arXiv:2505.12574) — must distinguish demote-clean-doc framing from the promote-attacker literature.
- Doc2Query-- "When Less is More" (2023) for over-expansion-hurts.
- CITATION ERROR TO FIX: packet cites 2504.21015 as "doc-expansion-hurts adds text to TARGET doc." Verified 2504.21015 is actually "Don't Retrieve, Generate: Prompting LLMs for Synthetic Training Data" (synthetic hard negatives) — a mismatch. 2604.05087 unverifiable (arXiv rate-limited; also future-dated). Prior-art table must be corrected before L1.

RATIONALE: Verified 2503.05037 (real, static biases — correctly excluded) and disproved the 2504.21015 citation (it is a synthetic-training-data paper, not document-expansion) via WebFetch — so the prior-art table has at least one wrong entry and rests partly on unverified 2026 IDs. Targeted searches (DuckDuckGo HTML + arXiv/ACL) for "term spam / keyword stuffing / corpus poisoning / over-expansion + BM25 length normalization + non-monotone" surfaced only (a) textbook length-norm/pivoted-norm material and (b) corpus-poisoning attacks that promote attacker docs — none reporting a clean third-party rank inverted-U vs competitor template length. So: no ≥2-source collision (rules out RED), but the mechanism delta over Singhal-1996/Robertson-2009 is thin and the IDF-collapse arm is self-conceded inactive on the L axis, and three to four mandatory references are missing/miscited (rules out GREEN). YELLOW: the specific observable is plausibly unoccupied and worth an L1, but only after the prior-art table is corrected, the length-norm-vs-TF (not IDF) crossover is restated honestly, and the work is positioned against pivoted-length-normalization + corpus-poisoning literature.
